WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.260 --> 00:00:04.300
Okay, so one of the most
interesting kind of weirdest

00:00:04.300 --> 00:00:07.130
work from home stories has
to be this company called,

00:00:07.130 --> 00:00:08.940
Relativity Space.

00:00:08.940 --> 00:00:12.980
They are a rocket startup
in Southern California,

00:00:12.980 --> 00:00:16.410
just outside of downtown Los Angeles.

00:00:16.410 --> 00:00:21.410
They are actually trying to
3D print an entire rocket

00:00:22.330 --> 00:00:24.480
using these giant machines

00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:27.260
that are sort of Westworld-like.

00:00:27.260 --> 00:00:28.780
In the midst of this pandemic

00:00:28.780 --> 00:00:30.450
and the work from home situation,

00:00:30.450 --> 00:00:33.150
their technology actually
looks like an advantage,

00:00:33.150 --> 00:00:35.310
a major advantage in
that they've been able

00:00:35.310 --> 00:00:38.130
to keep running these robots autonomously,

00:00:38.130 --> 00:00:40.550
just having one person go by the factory

00:00:40.550 --> 00:00:42.550
every now and again,
to keep the robots fed.

00:00:42.550 --> 00:00:45.280
To explain how all this works
and the company's vision,

00:00:45.280 --> 00:00:46.910
We're gonna talk to Tim Ellis,

00:00:46.910 --> 00:00:50.920
who is the co founder
and CEO of Relativity.

00:01:01.790 --> 00:01:04.290
I obviously, I cover the
space industry really close,

00:01:04.290 --> 00:01:06.210
and it's this very exciting time.

00:01:06.210 --> 00:01:08.480
Space X just had a historic launch.

00:01:08.480 --> 00:01:11.340
And then there's this flood
of other rocket startups

00:01:11.340 --> 00:01:12.173
that are coming.

00:01:12.173 --> 00:01:13.640
And I've been following
you guys for a while,

00:01:13.640 --> 00:01:14.967
but you guys are a little bit different

00:01:14.967 --> 00:01:16.930
than the other rocket startups.

00:01:16.930 --> 00:01:19.410
Can you sort of explain
what Relativity does.

00:01:19.410 --> 00:01:23.900
I founded Relativity back
at the beginning of 2016.

00:01:23.900 --> 00:01:26.730
And my co founder was
actually working at Space X

00:01:26.730 --> 00:01:28.350
on the Crew Dragon Program.

00:01:28.350 --> 00:01:30.030
I was at Blue Origin.

00:01:30.030 --> 00:01:32.180
The problem we saw in the industry is that

00:01:32.180 --> 00:01:35.110
really for the last 60 years,
no one's actually changed

00:01:35.110 --> 00:01:38.060
how rockets are manufactured.

00:01:38.060 --> 00:01:39.200
There's re-usability.

00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:41.300
there's other rocket technologies,

00:01:41.300 --> 00:01:44.310
which are new and very disruptive,

00:01:44.310 --> 00:01:46.890
but we really felt 3D
printing a whole rocket

00:01:46.890 --> 00:01:49.931
was gonna be the next big
disruption in the industry.

00:01:49.931 --> 00:01:52.480
If you go somewhere even like Space X,

00:01:52.480 --> 00:01:56.020
which is a super modern aerospace company,

00:01:56.020 --> 00:01:57.900
they do 3D print some stuff.

00:01:57.900 --> 00:01:59.810
It is particularly around the engines,

00:01:59.810 --> 00:02:01.640
but a lot of stuff is done by hand.

00:02:01.640 --> 00:02:02.520
Yes, that's right.

00:02:02.520 --> 00:02:04.360
Other companies we've seen are probably

00:02:04.360 --> 00:02:05.880
in the single digits,

00:02:05.880 --> 00:02:09.240
maybe as low as 0.1% of the rocket

00:02:09.240 --> 00:02:11.660
is actually 3D printed by mass.

00:02:11.660 --> 00:02:15.730
We're actually targeting
printing 95% of our rocket,

00:02:15.730 --> 00:02:18.740
so almost everything other
than the electronics.

00:02:18.740 --> 00:02:22.370
We're targeting, not just
printing 95% of the rocket,

00:02:22.370 --> 00:02:26.660
but doing it from raw
material to a finished rocket,

00:02:26.660 --> 00:02:28.850
in the factory in only 60 days.

00:02:28.850 --> 00:02:30.990
And that compares to about two years

00:02:30.990 --> 00:02:32.630
in a traditional factory.

00:02:32.630 --> 00:02:35.050
The 3D printers that
we ended up developing

00:02:35.050 --> 00:02:38.390
entirely in house use a big robots

00:02:38.390 --> 00:02:41.160
that come from industrial car automation

00:02:41.160 --> 00:02:43.540
or other sort of industrial automation.

00:02:43.540 --> 00:02:46.530
And then we've actually had
to write our own software.

00:02:46.530 --> 00:02:48.680
And then all of the control algorithms

00:02:48.680 --> 00:02:52.350
to basically, wherever
the robot moves around,

00:02:52.350 --> 00:02:54.680
we have a metal wire that gets fed in,

00:02:54.680 --> 00:02:59.280
and then we actually use
plasma to melt the wire,

00:02:59.280 --> 00:03:01.000
and then that solidifies.

00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:04.390
And so essentially now
we can code the robots

00:03:04.390 --> 00:03:07.190
using pretty sophisticated controls.

00:03:07.190 --> 00:03:08.930
Wherever the robot moves around,

00:03:08.930 --> 00:03:11.490
we can actually print our fuel tank.

00:03:11.490 --> 00:03:13.183
And it's very, very fast.

00:03:14.680 --> 00:03:16.850
As far as like why somebody
hasn't done this before.

00:03:16.850 --> 00:03:18.280
I remember when you guys got started.

00:03:18.280 --> 00:03:20.890
I think even still today,
maybe it's fair to say,

00:03:20.890 --> 00:03:24.180
I mean, there's people who are
skeptical of this approach.

00:03:24.180 --> 00:03:25.180
Obviously there's rockets there.

00:03:25.180 --> 00:03:26.490
It's a controlled explosion.

00:03:26.490 --> 00:03:29.800
Can you 3D print something
that's stable enough?

00:03:29.800 --> 00:03:31.860
We've actually already
produced a full scale,

00:03:31.860 --> 00:03:34.590
second stage structure
with the common dome

00:03:34.590 --> 00:03:37.610
and done proof testing to show
that it can hold the pressure

00:03:37.610 --> 00:03:39.530
and stresses of flight.

00:03:39.530 --> 00:03:42.010
Then we've done something
like 300 engine tests

00:03:42.010 --> 00:03:44.040
to date, all 3D printed.

00:03:44.040 --> 00:03:46.143
So we're actually proving the tech works.

00:03:48.160 --> 00:03:49.323
During the pandemic,

00:03:50.280 --> 00:03:52.480
I know a lot of the aerospace companies

00:03:52.480 --> 00:03:55.115
were able to stay relatively open

00:03:55.115 --> 00:03:57.530
if they were doing sort of stuff that had

00:03:57.530 --> 00:04:00.250
like a national security
kind of implication

00:04:00.250 --> 00:04:01.400
or national interest.

00:04:01.400 --> 00:04:02.760
What was it like for you guys?

00:04:02.760 --> 00:04:05.630
Have you been able to have
employees in the factory?

00:04:05.630 --> 00:04:08.220
We actually took a
pretty proactive approach

00:04:08.220 --> 00:04:12.460
to the pandemic, so around
March 6th to March 9th,

00:04:12.460 --> 00:04:15.570
we actually started
making everyone go home

00:04:15.570 --> 00:04:16.980
and work from home.

00:04:16.980 --> 00:04:18.910
So people unplugged their desktop.

00:04:18.910 --> 00:04:21.420
So we're actually a very
cloud forward company

00:04:21.420 --> 00:04:22.920
in our architecture,

00:04:22.920 --> 00:04:27.150
which lets us operate the
3D printers remotely anyway.

00:04:27.150 --> 00:04:29.860
So we were actually very
well prepped for that,

00:04:29.860 --> 00:04:33.140
prioritizing the health and
safety of our employees.

00:04:33.140 --> 00:04:34.910
We are deemed essential business,

00:04:34.910 --> 00:04:37.860
but we actually never had
to have more than one person

00:04:37.860 --> 00:04:40.180
in the factory at a time to keep printing.

00:04:40.180 --> 00:04:43.940
And we've also seen a very
minimal supply chain disruptions,

00:04:43.940 --> 00:04:46.310
because we have extremely few suppliers.

00:04:46.310 --> 00:04:49.387
There's really just not that
many processes that we need

00:04:49.387 --> 00:04:52.181
to build a rocket since
we're printing everything.

00:04:52.181 --> 00:04:54.550
We have very few raw materials.

00:04:54.550 --> 00:04:59.170
It's actually only three raw
metals for the entire rocket.

00:04:59.170 --> 00:05:01.800
It's so, so Elan's long stated goal is

00:05:01.800 --> 00:05:05.320
to eventually create a
human colony on Mars,

00:05:05.320 --> 00:05:08.460
and I know you have Mars
aspirations as well.

00:05:08.460 --> 00:05:10.600
I mean reflected on why Jordan

00:05:10.600 --> 00:05:12.210
and I had started the company.

00:05:12.210 --> 00:05:16.801
We had this core theory that
Space X was going to Mars.

00:05:16.801 --> 00:05:19.870
I'm actually super confident
that they're going to,

00:05:19.870 --> 00:05:22.315
and I'm really inspired by that.

00:05:22.315 --> 00:05:27.315
But every animation I saw
had the astronauts fly there

00:05:27.360 --> 00:05:29.810
and then they would land,

00:05:29.810 --> 00:05:31.420
and then the ladder would roll down,

00:05:31.420 --> 00:05:32.940
and then it would fade to black.

00:05:32.940 --> 00:05:37.477
And that became this thing
that stuck in my mind that,

00:05:37.477 --> 00:05:39.507
"Man there need to be dozens to hundreds

00:05:39.507 --> 00:05:41.577
"of companies working on different pieces

00:05:41.577 --> 00:05:43.330
"of the puzzle of going to Mars."

00:05:43.330 --> 00:05:46.650
And so that's where we came up
with our vision as a company,

00:05:46.650 --> 00:05:50.130
which is to 3D print the
first rocket made on Mars.

00:05:50.130 --> 00:05:52.680
I'm sure rocket's not the
first thing we're gonna make.

00:05:52.680 --> 00:05:55.115
We're probably gonna
make water storage tanks,

00:05:55.115 --> 00:05:58.250
habitat vessels, spare parts,

00:05:58.250 --> 00:06:03.250
other things that just help
early settlements get going.

00:06:03.540 --> 00:06:04.840
How old are you now?

00:06:04.840 --> 00:06:06.344
I just turned 30--

00:06:06.344 --> 00:06:07.680
Just turned 30--

00:06:07.680 --> 00:06:09.760
A few weeks ago, yup.

00:06:09.760 --> 00:06:12.290
And so what age do you expect to be

00:06:12.290 --> 00:06:16.993
when you 3D print your first
water storage tank on Mars?

00:06:16.993 --> 00:06:21.050
Yeah, honestly, I think it's
actually gonna happen faster

00:06:21.050 --> 00:06:24.520
than some of the human space flight can.

00:06:24.520 --> 00:06:26.070
So I think it could actually happen

00:06:26.070 --> 00:06:28.000
within a decade, honestly.

00:06:28.000 --> 00:06:30.785
My life philosophy has been

00:06:30.785 --> 00:06:33.590
if the future's not really
happening fast enough,

00:06:33.590 --> 00:06:37.291
well, why don't we just create it?

00:06:37.291 --> 00:06:40.803
We could actually be that
company that makes this happen.

